  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a maternal-to-infant microbiota pathway and contrasts a “healthy microbiota” with an “EED-associated microbiota,” likely referring to environmental enteric dysfunction. On both sides, a pregnant woman and infant are linked by arrows, suggesting early-life transfer or shaping of the child’s gut microbial community from the mother. The central comparison shows two circular microbial communities: the healthy state contains a mixed set of differently colored and shaped microbes, while the EED-associated state appears shifted toward a different composition, with more bright blue organisms and an altered balance among bacterial types.\n\nBelow, a shared bracket connects this microbiota difference to major child health outcomes. The listed areas are oral vaccine responses, immunity and infection, cognitive development, and growth and metabolism. Taken together, the visual message is that changes in the infant gut microbiota associated with EED are not just compositional differences but are linked to broad developmental and physiological consequences. The image emphasizes the microbiota as a central mediator between maternal/early-life exposures and multiple aspects of child health, rather than focusing on one single disease feature.",
